WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.952
Three financial statements answer different questions.

2
00:00:02.952 --> 00:00:07.434
The statement of financial position describes resources and obligations at a date.

3
00:00:07.434 --> 00:00:11.261
The operating statement describes revenues and expenses over a period.

4
00:00:11.261 --> 00:00:13.994
The cash-flow statement explains why cash changed.

5
00:00:13.994 --> 00:00:26.440
This episode adapts Kioko and Marlowe's chapter The Basic Financial Statements, published by University of Washington Pressbooks under Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 except where otherwise noted.

6
00:00:26.440 --> 00:00:31.114
It condenses, paraphrases, and reorganizes the authors' explanatory prose.

7
00:00:31.114 --> 00:00:37.432
Separately credited cases, examples, figures, tables, quotations, and linked materials are excluded.

8
00:00:37.432 --> 00:00:39.242
Financial position is a stock.

9
00:00:39.242 --> 00:00:45.699
Assets represent controlled resources, liabilities represent obligations, and net position is the residual.

10
00:00:45.699 --> 00:00:54.510
Classification matters because cash available for current bills is not the same as a restricted asset or a capital asset used to provide services.

11
00:00:54.510 --> 00:00:56.491
The operating statement is a flow.

12
00:00:56.491 --> 00:01:03.541
Under accrual accounting, revenue and expense recognition follow economic activity rather than only the movement of cash.

13
00:01:03.541 --> 00:01:07.561
Depreciation allocates the cost of a long-lived asset across periods.

14
00:01:07.561 --> 00:01:12.339
Receivables and payables connect current activity to cash collected or paid later.

15
00:01:12.339 --> 00:01:14.420
Cash flow restores the timing view.

16
00:01:14.420 --> 00:01:19.890
Operating activity, capital investment, and financing can move cash in different directions.

17
00:01:19.890 --> 00:01:26.905
An organization may report a favorable operating result while cash falls because it acquires equipment or repays debt.

18
00:01:26.905 --> 00:01:32.018
It may gain cash through borrowing without improving its underlying operating balance.

19
00:01:32.018 --> 00:01:36.311
Government reporting may also present more than one measurement focus.

20
00:01:36.311 --> 00:01:45.143
Government-wide statements emphasize the broader economic position, while fund statements organize legally and operationally distinct resources.

21
00:01:45.143 --> 00:01:51.889
A reader should identify the reporting entity, basis of accounting, period, and fund before comparing numbers.

22
00:01:51.889 --> 00:01:55.017
Reconciliations explain why two valid views differ.

23
00:01:55.017 --> 00:01:56.776
Torrance application segment.

24
00:01:56.776 --> 00:02:00.839
This original local reading exercise is separate from the textbook.

25
00:02:00.839 --> 00:02:12.665
It cites the City of Torrance Audit Reports page, checked October seventh, twenty twenty-six, at torrance C A dot gov slash Government slash Departments Offices slash Finance slash Audit Reports.

26
00:02:12.665 --> 00:02:18.911
The page lists Annual Comprehensive Financial Reports and separately audited statements by fiscal year.

27
00:02:18.911 --> 00:02:27.426
For a hypothetical review, select one annual comprehensive financial report from the official index and record its fiscal year before opening it.

28
00:02:27.426 --> 00:02:35.061
Identify the government-wide statements, major-fund statements, cash-flow statements where applicable, notes, and reconciliations.

29
00:02:35.061 --> 00:02:40.640
For every observation, preserve the statement name, reporting unit, date, and accounting basis.

30
00:02:40.640 --> 00:02:45.221
Do not combine a citywide net-position figure with a single-fund cash balance.

31
00:02:45.221 --> 00:02:51.211
This exercise teaches document navigation and reaches no conclusion about Torrance's financial health.

32
00:02:51.211 --> 00:02:54.445
Read the statements together and compare multiple periods.

33
00:02:54.445 --> 00:03:04.982
Ask whether reported results are recurring, whether liquid resources cover near-term claims, whether capital assets are being maintained, and whether liabilities are shifting costs forward.

34
00:03:04.982 --> 00:03:09.554
Notes and accounting policies are part of the statements, not optional fine print.

35
00:03:09.554 --> 00:03:17.243
